<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kelmis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as La Calamine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kelmis, in French La Calamine, is a municipality in the German-speaking Community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>. Its zinc mine, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vieille-montagne">Vieille Montagne</a>, was so valuable that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> left it in the neutral condominium of Neutral Moresnet from 1816 until the territory passed to Belgium after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Mining there ended in 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kelmis.jpg" alt="Kelmis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. David Edgar assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KelmisView.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
